Ascites (Malignant)
Palliative & End of Life
Progressive abdominal distension, Shifting dullness, Refractory to diuretic therapy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-013
Reason for Visit: Acute distress secondary to massive, tense abdominal distension causing severe dyspnea and intractable vomiting.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, adult son
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient was experiencing severe, rapidly progressive abdominal distension over the last 48 hours, resulting in significant mechanical dyspnea and inability to keep oral fluids down due to forceful vomiting. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to evaluate the degree of respiratory compromise, provide immediate symptom relief for the severe nausea, and assess the need for urgent therapeutic paracentesis prior to the next regular clinic day.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach is stretched so tight I feel like I'm going to burst, and I can't even take a deep breath without throwing up."
History of Present Illness: Over the past two weeks, the patient has developed severe, progressive, and highly uncomfortable global abdominal distension completely out of proportion to their overall body weight. There is rapid, profound, and distinctly unintentional significant weight gain specifically despite severe underlying systemic cachexia. The patient reports intractable, severe early satiety, profound nausea, and frequent completely unremitting forceful vomiting starting yesterday. Today, this progressed to severe dyspnea explicitly secondary strictly to massive mechanical upward diaphragmatic displacement and subsequent restricted lung expansion. The patient has shown a refractory, explicit complete lack of clinical response to high-dose standardized loop and strictly potassium-sparing diuretic therapy over the last 10 days.
Functional Status: Bedbound today; completely unable to sit forward or mobilize due to the tense, heavy abdomen.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of dehydration due to intractable vomiting; severe discomfort preventing basic hygiene tasks.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 26 bpm | SpO2: 94% on room air | Wt: 75 Kg (up 6kg in 2 weeks)
General: Cachectic patient with a massively distended abdomen, in obvious respiratory distress, using accessory muscles to breathe.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals tense, explicitly shiny abdominal skin frequently presenting with distinct, visible dilated superficial collateral veins. Eversion of the umbilicus is distinctly driven completely by severely profoundly elevated intra-abdominal explicitly localized pressure. Shifting dullness is objectively demonstrated strictly upon precise percussion of the explicitly lateral abdominal flanks, and a fluid thrill is explicitly palpable strictly traversing directly across the severe, profoundly distended abdominal cavity. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the observed progressive abdominal distension, positive shifting dullness, and the medication history confirming the condition is refractory to diuretic therapy.
Environmental Observations: Hospital bed is appropriately positioned, but the patient cannot tolerate lying flat at all.
Medication Reconciliation: Oral furosemide and spironolactone have been vomited repeatedly today, rendering them ineffective.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Ascites (Malignant) - Status: Exacerbated (Severe mechanical compromise)
Differentials:

Small bowel obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of shifting dullness and fluid thrill indicating massive free fluid, rather than purely tympanic, gas-filled distension, though partial compression may contribute to the nausea.

Acute urinary retention - Ruled out/unlikely due to the global, diffuse nature of the distension reaching the epigastrium, and the patient has voided small amounts of concentrated urine recently.

Liver failure (non-malignant cirrhosis) - Ruled out/unlikely given the known history of advanced peritoneal carcinomatosis, which is the primary driver of this exudative ascites.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe mechanical crisis driven by massive malignant ascites. The extreme intra-abdominal pressure is directly causing profound diaphragmatic splinting (resulting in tachypnea and hypoxia) and severe gastric compression (resulting in intractable vomiting). Because oral medications are entirely failing and the respiratory compromise is worsening, delaying assessment until standard hours would subject the patient to agonizing, suffocating distress and a high risk of aspiration from continuous vomiting. Immediate after-hours medical assessment was absolutely critical to bypass the oral route with parenteral antiemetics and arrange urgent, definitive fluid drainage.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat subcutaneous haloperidol and dexamethasone to rapidly manage the centrally-driven and mechanically-driven nausea. Instructed the patient to remain strictly positioned at a 45-degree angle to optimize diaphragmatic excursion. Arranged an urgent referral for an ultrasound-guided therapeutic paracentesis at the local ambulatory care unit first thing in the morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the pressure against your lungs increasing (trigger), explicitly use the electric bed controls to raise the head of the bed to at least 45 degrees (behavior). This will allow gravity to pull the fluid downward, immediately giving your lungs more room to expand and reducing the sensation of breathlessness (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the community palliative care team to coordinate the ambulatory paracentesis.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensure an emesis bag and oral care supplies are constantly within reach to prevent leaning forward, which exacerbates the pain.
Follow-up: Ambulatory care unit review tomorrow for definitive fluid drainage; GP review post-procedure.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops sudden, severe, localized abdominal pain with a high fever (suggesting spontaneous bacterial peritonitis), or if the breathing becomes so compromised that the patient is gasping or turning blue despite sitting fully upright, the son must call 000 immediately for emergency transfer.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The medications provided will only ease the nausea; definitive relief of the breathing and pressure requires the fluid to be physically drained.
Clinical Course: Malignant ascites frequently reaccumulates rapidly despite drainage. A permanent indwelling peritoneal catheter may need to be discussed in the near future.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to lie flat under any circumstances. If the vomiting persists despite the injection, or if breathing becomes severely labored, call the after-hours triage line immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The cancer has caused a massive amount of fluid to leak into your belly. This fluid is squashing your stomach, making you vomit, and squashing your lungs, making you breathless. The injection will stop the vomiting center in your brain, but we must urgently arrange to drain the fluid tomorrow to fix the actual pressure.
Actionable Steps: Keep the bed elevated. Take only tiny sips of clear fluids; do not attempt solid food until the fluid is drained.

